#!/usr/bin/env etcs

# ONE CANVAS: the sheet, with the toolbar drawn on top at the bottom edge.
#
# The toolbar is a CompositeDrawable2D child of sheet_root (order 20), not a
# second Surface / DOM element. Every pointer event comes through the window's
# GLFW stream; a press on a swatch is a pick inside the same tree as the picture.

spawn RenderProvider::Instance gpu
gpu.Create()

spawn WindowProvider::Window main
main.Create(1024, 768, ETCS Paint)

# ── the sheet ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
main.spawn(RenderProvider::Surface view)
view.Create(@gpu)
view.Clear(0.13, 0.13, 0.15, 1.0)

# ── the root, the throbber and the edge go up FIRST ──────────────────────────
#
# What a frame edge presents is whatever is bound at the time, and the root it
# presents is the same sheet_root the rest of this file fills in -- so all three
# go up here and the scene appears inside a picture that is already being drawn.
# Detaching the edge on the last line instead leaves the canvas blank for the
# whole boot, which is the one interval a wait indicator is for.
#
# ONE ROOT, NOT A SEPARATE BOOT ROOT: a boot root has to be swapped out, which
# leaves the throbber in a tree nothing presents afterwards, and a wait
# indicator that cannot be shown again is one every later caller rebuilds. In
# the real root it stays available for the whole session.
#
# Building a tree while the edge walks it is ordinary here, not a liberty: the
# layer panel spawns and deletes its rows on every Refresh with this same edge
# running, and the panes below are shown and hidden the same way.

# THE STAGE: what the frame edge presents, and the only reason it exists is that
# "the scene" and "the throbber" have to be hidden and shown independently.
#
# A node has one parent, so the throbber cannot be inside the scene it covers
# AND survive the scene being hidden. One level of wrapper solves it and nothing
# else does: stage holds exactly two children, and each is switched on its own.
#
# sheet_root keeps its identity, its size, its position and every reference to
# it (BindRoot, AddPane below) -- it is one level deeper and still at 0,0, so
# no coordinate anywhere moves.
spawn RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D stage
stage.Create(1024, 768)
stage.SetPosition(0, 0)
stage.SetOrder(0)
stage.SetBackground(0.13, 0.13, 0.15, 1.0)

stage.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D sheet_root)
sheet_root.Create(1024, 768)
sheet_root.SetPosition(0, 0)
sheet_root.SetOrder(0)

# HIDDEN UNTIL IT IS FINISHED, which is the whole point of the wrapper.
# Composing the scene from the top and letting it fill in shows: panes appearing
# one at a time, a canvas half built, a toolbar arriving after the picture. A
# wait indicator exists to say "not yet", and a scene assembling itself
# underneath one says the opposite.
#
# Hidden is also not picked (ontology/DrawableBase.h), so a click during boot
# cannot land on a control that is not ready to answer it.
sheet_root.SetHidden(1)

# THE SESSION'S THROBBER, and the name is the point.
#
# A sibling of the scene rather than a child of it, so hiding one does not hide
# the other -- which is the reason `stage` exists at all.
#
# Spawned once, here, because this is the first thing that needs one, and kept
# afterwards rather than deleted, so anything else with a wait in it (a new
# canvas, a page switch, an import) shows THIS one instead of building another.
# Two lines, from any subscript:
#
#     ensure RenderProvider::Throbber main_throbber
#     main_throbber.SetHidden(0)
#
# ENSURE, NOT SPAWN, and the difference is the whole point of the name. The
# three acquire verbs differ only in what happens when the word is already
# answered (core/CommandExecutor.h, CmdAcquire):
#
#     spawn    always makes a new one, and REFUSES if the name already
#              resolves -- "clobbering ... did you mean attach/ensure?"
#     attach   takes the existing one, and fails if there is not one
#     ensure   takes the existing one if there is one, makes one if not
#
# So a subscript that says `spawn` is the failure this arrangement exists to
# avoid: it errors out in exactly the session where the throbber is already
# there. `ensure` reuses it here and still works standalone. Same shape
# NetworkProvider/scripts/chess_web.etcs already uses for the shared server
# (`ensure NetworkProvider::HttpServer web`).
#
# WHAT ENSURE GUARANTEES IS AN ENTITY, NOT A PICTURE. It resolves through the
# closure, so this binding is reachable because it is made by the session's root
# script; one made by `ensure` in a standalone subscript is parented wherever
# that script's root is, which may be nothing the compose root walks. A
# subscript that has to work either way places what it gets.
#
# No SetOrder: the type defaults to the top (Throbber::ORDER_ON_TOP), because
# for a wait indicator that is not a layout choice but what the thing is.
# 464,336 is (1024-96)/2, (768-96)/2: the raster is exactly the ring's square
# (the caption sits inside it), so this is the centre of the sheet.
stage.spawn(RenderProvider::Throbber main_throbber)
main_throbber.Create(96)
main_throbber.SetPosition(464, 336)

# HIDDEN FROM THE START, because nothing in here CAN draw during boot: the
# runtime's first presented frame lands seconds after this script starts,
# whatever the script contains (bootThrobber in www/index.html has the
# measurement). The page covers that interval with the same twelve dots in the
# same two colours. THIS one is for the waits that happen afterwards, when the
# runtime is up and can draw: a new page, a page switch, a load (pages.BindWait
# below). Hidden, it costs one virtual call a frame.
main_throbber.SetHidden(1)

view.Compose(@stage)

# THE EDGE, and there is exactly one of it.
detach RenderProvider/scripts/render_frames.etcs view=view


# ── the document: paper under ink ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The layers are the document's CHILDREN, which is what makes them its layers --
# membership is parenthood, so the stack is read out of the typed-children list
# that already holds them rather than out of a second list saying the same thing.
spawn PaintProvider::PaintDocument doc
doc.Create(1024, 768, browser)

doc.spawn(PaintProvider::PaintLayer paper)
paper.Create(1024, 768)
paper.Clear(1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
paper.SetName(Paper)
paper.SetOrder(0)

doc.spawn(PaintProvider::PaintLayer ink)
ink.Create(1024, 768)
ink.Clear(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
ink.SetName(Ink)
ink.SetOrder(1)

doc.SetActiveLayer(@ink)

# The document draws its own text boxes, so it needs a glyph provider of its own
# -- see PaintTextBox. A label spawned for nothing but its Glyphs half: it is
# never positioned or drawn, it is asked to rasterise into somebody else's pixels.
spawn RenderProvider::TextLabel glyph_src
glyph_src.Create()
doc.BindGlyphs(@glyph_src)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintTool brush
brush.SetRadius(6.0)
brush.SetColor(0.10, 0.10, 0.12, 1.0)


# ── the sheet's own pane ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A popup over the canvas needs a 2D tree: a pane has to exist for the wheel to
# be ON TOP of, and the wheel has to be a pane for opening it to mean anything
# (PaintColorWheel: open is membership of the routing set, because a drawable
# that is merely invisible still swallows clicks).
#
# So the sheet gets a root at order 0, the same size as the document, and
# BindCanvas names it as the thing that IS the picture -- anything else picked
# inside it is scenery and does not draw. It is made at the TOP of this file
# now, with the throbber in it, because the frame edge presents it from there
# on; only its contents are filled in from here down.

# ── the drawable pane, INSET, so the ruler has somewhere to be ───────────────
#
# THE MARGIN IS THE POINT, and it is 36 px on three sides. A ruler drawn along
# the inside of the drawing area is on the picture: over the paper near the
# edges, and paintable, so a stroke near a corner goes through the scale you are
# checking it against. Inset, the band between this pane and sheet_root belongs
# to nobody who draws -- so the marks go there (canvas.BindRulerFrame) and cannot
# be drawn on.
#
# AND NOTHING HAS TO ENFORCE THAT, which is why it is a pane rather than a rule.
# The router hit-tests panes and PaintInput only treats a hit on the CANVAS node
# as the picture (BindCanvas, below): a point in the margin is inside sheet_root
# and outside this, so it reaches the toolbar's pick and never reaches a tool. The
# document cannot spill into the band either -- this pane is its own raster, so
# the blit is clipped by the buffer's extent rather than by a check.
#
# 704 rather than 768 on the bottom because the toolbar has that strip; the
# ruler's own band sizes itself per side and simply draws nothing where a page
# left no room (draw_edge_ruler).
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D paper_pane)
paper_pane.Create(952, 632)
paper_pane.SetPosition(36, 36)
paper_pane.SetOrder(1)
# RETAINED: PaintSurface::Render is the one writer of this buffer and clears it
# itself before blitting the document in. A composite normally clears to its
# background before drawing its children, because normally the buffer is
# derived from the tree; here the buffer IS the picture, and a recompose that
# cleared it would wipe the document -- the sheet going blank whenever anything
# is composed over it.
paper_pane.SetRetain(1)

# ── the document draws INTO the pane, not beside it ──────────────────────────
#
# This is the one ordering that works, and the alternative is worth stating
# because it looks right and is not. Leaving the document pointed at `view` and
# composing the tree into the same surface gives two writers: the frame pump
# rebuilds `view` from the tree every frame, which wipes whatever Render blitted.
#
# So the pane is the TARGET. canvas clears and blits the document into
# paper_pane's own raster (a CompositeDrawable2D is a Surface and owns Pixels),
# the compose walk then draws the sheet's other children -- the ruler, the
# window, the bar -- over it in order, and `view` presents the one composed
# result. ONE WRITER PER BUFFER: the surface writes the pane and the ruler's
# raster, the walk writes everything else, and a widget is over the picture
# because the tree says so rather than because of which call ran last.
spawn PaintProvider::PaintSurface canvas
canvas.Create(@paper_pane)
canvas.AttachDocument(@doc)
canvas.SetBackground(0.13, 0.13, 0.15, 1.0)
# For the edge ruler's numbers -- a second consumer of the same provider the
# document uses, because the ruler describes the PANE and the boxes the page.
canvas.BindGlyphs(@glyph_src)
# ── the ruler, ON ITS OWN RASTER ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The margin around the pane is where the ruler goes (the inset above), and
# it is drawn by PaintSurface::Render -- on the INPUT thread, whenever the
# view changes, which during a drag or a stroke is per motion. It used to be
# drawn straight into sheet_root's buffer, which the frame edge composes and
# presents on ITS thread. Two writers, no order between them: the band's clear
# landed over the toolbar's rows (the band runs to the sheet's edge, under the
# bar) and over anything dragged across the margin, and whether the presented
# frame showed the band or the bar depended on which thread got there last.
# Measured: a third of the frames during a drag showed the band over the bar.
#
# So the ruler has a raster of its own, a sibling of the pane at the sheet's
# origin and the sheet's size, and the surface writes THAT. The sheet is then
# what a compositor is meant to be -- derived from its children, in order:
# pane (1), ruler (2), the layer window (15), the bar (19..21), popups (29..30)
# -- and the bar is over the band because the tree says so, every frame, with
# nobody's write racing it. What can still tear is the ruler's own raster, a
# band half-updated between two presents, which is the same band in the same
# colour and does not show.
#
# Retained: nothing in the tree draws it, so nothing may clear it either; the
# surface's band rects cover the margin and the area under the pane is never
# touched, so it stays transparent from Allocate. Order 2, just over the pane:
# the ruler is chrome, and everything else is over chrome.
#
# NOT PICKABLE, and this is the line that makes a full-sheet sibling possible:
# the pick walks children top down and a compositor answers for its whole
# rectangle, so without it every press on the paper would have landed on the
# ruler and no stroke would ever have started (CompositeDrawable2D::SetPickable).
# It is at the sheet's origin so the pane's Bounds() -- in the sheet's space --
# are the same offsets in the ruler's space, which is what draw_edge_ruler
# reads (PaintSurface::BindRulerFrame).
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D ruler_pane)
ruler_pane.Create(1024, 768)
ruler_pane.SetPosition(0, 0)
ruler_pane.SetOrder(2)
ruler_pane.SetRetain(1)
ruler_pane.SetPickable(0)
# THE ONE PANE THAT IS NOT A LAYOUT BOX. It is the sheet, at the sheet's
# origin, overlapping the picture -- which is not a row or a column and so is
# not something a solver has anything to say about. It follows the window
# straight (CompositeDrawable2D::FollowResize), and the equality the comment
# above depends on -- the pane's offsets in the sheet's space are the same in
# this one's -- holds at every size because both are the window's.
ruler_pane.FollowResize(@main)
canvas.BindRulerFrame(@ruler_pane)
canvas.Render()

# NOT RETAINED, and the sheet needs no line to say so: with the ruler on its
# own raster nothing but the compose walk writes this buffer, so it is cleared
# and rebuilt from the tree like any other compositor. That is also what ends
# the re-Render a popup's close, a window drag and the wheel's move used to
# ask for: whatever a moved thing was covering is redrawn by the walk, because
# the walk draws everything.

# ── the toolbar, ON the sheet ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE BACKING STRIP IS GONE. It was a PolygonDrawable2D with 0/1024/704/768
# baked into its corners, which is geometry and not a position -- so it could
# not follow a resize, and at any width but 1024 it stopped painting before the
# sheet's edge. A compositor's own background is the same picture with nothing
# to keep in step, and the bar is one.
#
# Sixteen 60px slices: six colours, eight tools, radius and alpha
# (paint_toolbar.etcs) -- rect and oval are one shape slice now, which is what
# gave the row its margins back. The bar is a layout box from here on (the
# layout, at the foot of this file); the slices keep their 60px cells, so a
# wider bar has slack at the right end rather than stretched buttons.
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D bar)
bar.Create(960, 64)
bar.SetPosition(32, 704)
bar.SetOrder(20)
bar.SetBackground(0.16, 0.16, 0.19, 1.0)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintPalette palette
palette.BindTool(@brush)
palette.BindSurface(@canvas)

# THE SOLVER, SPAWNED HERE rather than at the foot of the file, because the
# toolbar declares its own cells into it (paint_toolbar.etcs) and a box has to
# have somewhere to be declared. Only Create is called now; the boxes this file
# owns, the first Solve and FollowResize are all at the end, once everything
# they name exists.
spawn LayoutProvider::Layout layout
layout.Create(1024, 768)

run PaintProvider/scripts/paint_toolbar.etcs bar=bar palette=palette layout=layout


# ── the zoom readout, in a row of its own under the picture ──────────────────
#
# Two steps and a percentage, deliberately faint: it is a thing you reach for
# occasionally and should not compete with the picture.
#
# IN A PANE, and the pane is why. These were children of sheet_root at y=668 --
# four pixels above a toolbar that was at 704 because the sheet was 768. Every
# one of those numbers is the old height read backwards, so a taller sheet left
# the row stranded in the middle of the picture. A pane is a layout box (the
# layout, at the foot of this file), so the row sits under the picture and over
# the bar at any height, and the coordinates below are its own 26px band rather
# than a subtraction from a size nothing guarantees.
#
# The steps are PaintPalette entries -- a node that means a zoom, mapped by the
# same type that maps a node meaning a colour (AddZoom says why a view setting
# lives there). The label is pushed by the surface rather than by whoever zoomed,
# so the wheel, these buttons and a script all keep it honest.
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D zoom_pane)
zoom_pane.Create(960, 26)
zoom_pane.SetPosition(36, 672)
zoom_pane.SetOrder(21)

zoom_pane.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D zoom_out)
zoom_out.Create()
zoom_out.AddPoint(0, 1)
zoom_out.AddPoint(24, 1)
zoom_out.AddPoint(24, 25)
zoom_out.AddPoint(0, 25)
zoom_out.SetFill(0.85, 0.85, 0.88, 0.30)
zoom_out.SetOrder(21)
palette.AddZoom(@zoom_out, 0.8)

zoom_pane.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D zoom_in)
zoom_in.Create()
zoom_in.AddPoint(32, 1)
zoom_in.AddPoint(56, 1)
zoom_in.AddPoint(56, 25)
zoom_in.AddPoint(32, 25)
zoom_in.SetFill(0.85, 0.85, 0.88, 0.30)
zoom_in.SetOrder(21)
palette.AddZoom(@zoom_in, 1.25)

zoom_pane.spawn(RenderProvider::TextLabel zoom_label)
zoom_label.Create()
zoom_label.SetPosition(66, 5)
zoom_label.SetSize(2)
zoom_label.SetColor(0.80, 0.80, 0.84, 1.0)
zoom_label.SetText(100%)
zoom_label.SetOrder(21)

canvas.BindZoomLabel(@zoom_label)

# ── the layer window, top-right of the paper ─────────────────────────────────
#
# A pane on the sheet like the toolbar, five rows of eye / name / delete built
# by paint_layers.etcs (the same bargain: PaintProvider maps nodes to layer
# actions and owns none of the drawing). Over the paper's corner rather than in
# the margin, because the margin is the ruler's and 36px is not a list. Picked
# through the sheet's own input: a press on a row reaches the panel before the
# canvas check (PaintInput::RouteEvent), so nothing is routed separately and no
# stroke starts under it. The active layer's row is the highlighted one, and a
# press on another row makes THAT the drawing layer.
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D layers_pane)
layers_pane.Create(240, 224)
layers_pane.SetPosition(740, 44)

# UNDER THE TOOLBAR, and that is the one ordering rule this window has that is
# not about z at all -- it is about what is furniture and what is a document
# view. The toolbar is fixed to the sheet; the layers panel is a window the user
# drags around. A dragged window passing over a fixed control and covering it
# reads as the control having gone away, which for a palette you are mid-stroke
# with is the wrong answer. 15 puts it above the picture (paper_pane is 1) and
# below the bar and everything mounted on it (bar_bg 19, bar 20, the zoom
# controls and the gear at 21).
#
# The popups stay ABOVE the toolbar (wheel 30, menu and import 29) on purpose:
# those are opened deliberately, are modal in intent, and close on a press
# elsewhere -- a thing you summoned should be on top of what you summoned it
# from. This panel is not summoned; it is simply there.
layers_pane.SetOrder(15)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintLayerPanel layers
run PaintProvider/scripts/paint_layers.etcs panel=layers_pane doc=doc list=layers
# What to put back when the window changes the picture rather than the list --
# a restack, an eye, a delete (PaintLayerPanel::BindSurface).
layers.BindSurface(@canvas)
layers.Refresh()

# THE CLOCK THE FADE RIDES IS NOT SPAWNED HERE ANY MORE. The isolation fade used
# to need a node in the tree -- a drawable that drew nothing, present only so the
# compose walk would reach it -- because the walk was the only per-frame edge in
# this module. The panel claims Animated now (ontology/Animated.h), so it is
# stepped by whatever drives that family and the window needs nothing beside it.

# ── the colour wheel's pane ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Order 30, above everything, because a popup that is not on top is not a popup.
# NOT added to the router here: the wheel does that when it opens, which is what
# makes "is it open" and "does it receive clicks" one fact instead of two that
# can disagree.
# A CHILD OF THE SHEET, which it was not, and that was the whole of why nobody
# had seen this wheel. Spawned at top level it had no drawable parent, so no
# compose walk ever reached it: order 30 ranks it against siblings it did not
# have. It was still ROUTED when open, so clicks over it were swallowed by an
# invisible panel -- the worst of both.
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D wheel_pane)
# 240 wide, not 220: the disc is 192 across and the brightness strip lives to the
# right of it (PaintColorWheel::in_value_strip derives its x from the radius).
wheel_pane.Create(240, 220)
wheel_pane.SetPosition(340, 260)
wheel_pane.SetOrder(30)
# Hidden until opened. It is a child of the sheet now, so without this it would
# be drawn over the picture from boot -- see PaintColorWheel::Open, which treats
# open as one fact covering both routing and appearance.
wheel_pane.SetHidden(1)

# RETAINED, and it has no children at all -- because the PICKER IS THE RASTER.
#
# Not twelve polygons drawn by a script over a backing rectangle -- the number
# twelve is the bug: the pick was continuous while the picture was
# not, so the colour you got was almost never the colour you clicked. Now
# PaintColorWheel paints the pane's own pixels -- hue around, saturation outward,
# a brightness strip down the right edge -- from exactly the conversion the pick
# runs. Retain is what stops the compose walk clearing it, and having no children
# is what stops anything drawing over it: one writer per buffer.
wheel_pane.SetRetain(1)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintColorWheel wheel
wheel.Create(110, 110, 96)
wheel.BindTool(@brush)
wheel.BindPalette(@palette)
wheel.BindSurface(@canvas)
wheel.SetValue(1.0)
# And the palette reaches back, so a swatch's own arrow can open the wheel above
# it and aim the pick at that swatch (PaintPalette::AddWheelArrow).
palette.BindWheel(@wheel)

# ── one input machine for the sheet (toolbar is scenery inside it) ──────────
spawn PaintProvider::PaintInput input
input.Create(@doc, @brush, @canvas)
input.SetBrush(6.0, 0.10, 0.10, 0.12, 1.0)
input.BindRoot(@sheet_root)
# THE PANE, NOT THE SHEET. This one line is what makes the ruler's margin
# unpaintable: a press in the band picks sheet_root, which is not the canvas, so
# it is scenery and no tool sees it (PaintInput::RouteEvent). The root stays the
# sheet so the toolbar and the zoom steps are still reachable by the same pick.
input.BindCanvas(@paper_pane)
input.BindPalette(@palette)
input.BindPanel(@layers)
input.BindWheel(@wheel)
input.SetWheelPane(0)

# ── the pages: this session's history of canvases, kept in a database ────────
#
# ONE FILE, ONE PATH, TWO SUBSTRATES. The database is sqlite at a path, here as
# on the desktop; in the browser the path is under /persist, which index.html
# mounts on IDBFS and syncs to IndexedDB, so it outlives the tab. Nothing about
# the store is web-specific, and nothing about it is paint-specific either: the
# pages type talks to it through the Database family (Prepare/Bind/Step), and
# any other module with bytes to keep can do the same.
#
# ctrl+PageDown / ctrl+PageUp switch pages: the present page is saved first if
# it changed since it was loaded (PaintDocument::revision), then the neighbour
# is loaded. `pages.New()` opens a fresh page; `pages.List()` says what is kept.
spawn DatabaseProvider::LocalDatabase db
db.Connect(/persist/pages.db)
spawn PaintProvider::PaintPages pages
pages.Create(@doc, @db)
pages.BindSurface(@canvas)
# Shown while a page is saved, loaded or made -- the one wait in this session
# long enough to see (PaintPages::BindWait has the numbers). This is what the
# throbber above was kept for.
pages.BindWait(@main_throbber)
input.BindPages(@pages)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintInput wheel_input
wheel_input.Create(@doc, @brush, @canvas)
wheel_input.BindRoot(@wheel_pane)
wheel_input.BindWheel(@wheel)
wheel_input.SetWheelPane(1)

# ── the arbiter ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Budget 1: the topmost pane containing the point, and nobody below it. That is
# what stops a press on the wheel from also landing on the picture underneath.
spawn PaintProvider::PaintRouter router
router.Create(1)
router.AddPane(@sheet_root, @input)

wheel.BindRouter(@router)
wheel.BindPane(@wheel_pane, @wheel_input)

# ── the gear, and the settings menu it opens ─────────────────────────────────
#
# IN THE RULER'S CORNER, above the extent label. The corner of the margin is the
# one place on the sheet that is neither picture nor toolbar nor scale: the band
# is 36px, its label sits at y=16 (draw_edge_ruler puts the extent at
# oy - band_h + 2), so an octagon in the 2..14 square above it overlaps nothing.
# A child of sheet_root like the zoom steps, ordered over the ruler's raster,
# so the band's clear never takes the gear with it.
#
# A press on it is scenery routed to `input` -- inside sheet_root, outside the
# canvas -- and PaintInput::RouteEvent asks the palette first, which is where
# AddPopup answers (PaintPalette::Apply). The palette needs the router to open
# anything, since open means routed (PaintColorWheel::Open).
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D gear)
gear.Create()
gear.AddPoint(8, 2)
gear.AddPoint(12, 4)
gear.AddPoint(14, 8)
gear.AddPoint(12, 12)
gear.AddPoint(8, 14)
gear.AddPoint(4, 12)
gear.AddPoint(2, 8)
gear.AddPoint(4, 4)
gear.SetFill(0.94, 0.89, 0.78, 0.92)
gear.SetOrder(21)

# THE WORD, so the dot says what it is. Same fill as the octagon, exactly: the
# association IS the colour, and a label in the ruler's ordinary ink would read
# as one more piece of margin furniture next to an unexplained dot.
#
# x=18 clears the octagon's 2..14 by four; y=4 centres a scale-1 cell (five
# columns, seven rows of face -- TextLabel::CELL_W/CELL_H) against its middle.
# Four glyphs at ADVANCE 6 is 23px, so this ends around x=41, well inside the
# margin and well above the extent label at y=16.
#
# BOUND TO THE SAME POPUP as the octagon, because a word beside a button that
# does nothing when pressed is worse than no word: the label is a sibling, not a
# child, so a press on it picks the LABEL and the palette has to know it opens
# the same thing. paint_menu.etcs does this for every control in the panel and
# says so on its own top comment; this is that convention, one level out.
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::TextLabel gear_word)
gear_word.Create()
gear_word.SetPosition(18, 4)
gear_word.SetSize(1)
gear_word.SetColor(0.94, 0.89, 0.78, 0.92)
gear_word.SetText(menu)
gear_word.SetOrder(21)

# The menu's pane: order 29, above everything but the wheel, so the two popups
# rank as popups against the sheet and the wheel still wins if both were open
# (the palette closes the wheel when it opens this, so they are not). Hidden
# until opened, for the reason wheel_pane is. Not retained: its contents are
# nodes (paint_menu.etcs) and the compositor draws them from the tree.
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D menu_pane)
menu_pane.Create(200, 372)
menu_pane.SetPosition(4, 18)
menu_pane.SetOrder(29)
menu_pane.SetBackground(0.106, 0.110, 0.078, 0.96)
menu_pane.SetHidden(1)

# The pending extent and anchor the menu edits, seeded from the document, and
# the surface it re-renders once a resize or a new canvas has landed.
spawn PaintProvider::PaintCanvasMenu menu
menu.Create(@doc)
menu.BindSurface(@canvas)

# Its own input, exactly as the wheel has one: a pane is a root to pick in and
# an input to hand the pick to (PaintRouter::AddPane), and this pane is not the
# sheet. The palette is bound because every control in the menu is a palette
# entry (AddCall) -- and because, with a popup open, the palette is what says a
# press on the pane's backing is the popup's and not a stroke (PaintPalette::
# Apply), so no BindCanvas is needed here.
spawn PaintProvider::PaintInput menu_input
menu_input.Create(@doc, @brush, @canvas)
menu_input.BindRoot(@menu_pane)
menu_input.BindPalette(@palette)

run PaintProvider/scripts/paint_menu.etcs pane=menu_pane palette=palette menu=menu

# The store the menu lists and that `new` adds to. Bound after the script has
# declared the rows, so the first RefreshPages has somewhere to write.
menu.BindPages(@pages)

palette.BindRouter(@router)
palette.AddPopup(@gear, @menu_pane, @menu_input)
palette.AddPopup(@gear_word, @menu_pane, @menu_input)

# ── who is in the session ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A SECOND POPUP, on the same machinery as the settings menu: its own pane, its
# own input, and the palette answering presses on it. Order 30 -- above the
# menu, because it is opened FROM a header button rather than from the gear and
# should not end up behind whatever was last up.
#
# Hidden until the page opens it (PaintVisitors.Open), which the page does the
# moment a session exists. Closing it is what ends the session: the runtime
# raises the event, the page tells the node, and every other page's next read
# answers NO SUCH SESSION and falls back to the canvas it already has.
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D visitors_pane)
visitors_pane.Create(300, 300)
visitors_pane.SetPosition(4, 18)
visitors_pane.SetOrder(30)
visitors_pane.SetBackground(0.106, 0.110, 0.078, 0.96)
visitors_pane.SetHidden(1)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintVisitors visitors
visitors.Create()
visitors.BindWindow(@visitors_pane)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintInput visitors_input
visitors_input.Create(@doc, @brush, @canvas)
visitors_input.BindRoot(@visitors_pane)
visitors_input.BindPalette(@palette)

run PaintProvider/scripts/paint_visitors.etcs pane=visitors_pane palette=palette visitors=visitors

# The pane joins the router the same way the wheel and the menu do -- a pane is
# a root to pick in and an input to hand the pick to. Registered here rather
# than as an AddPopup because nothing on the sheet OPENS it: the page does.
router.AddPane(@visitors_pane, @visitors_input)

# ── the import prompt ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A popup with no node to press for it: the page's upload hands the file's
# path to menu.OfferImport, which opens this pane and asks whether the picture
# is another layer or a new canvas (paint_import.etcs). Over the middle of the
# paper, order 29 like the menu, its own input for the reason menu_input is.
sheet_root.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D import_pane)
import_pane.Create(300, 90)
import_pane.SetPosition(398, 300)
import_pane.SetOrder(29)
import_pane.SetBackground(0.106, 0.110, 0.078, 0.96)
import_pane.SetHidden(1)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintInput import_input
import_input.Create(@doc, @brush, @canvas)
import_input.BindRoot(@import_pane)
import_input.BindPalette(@palette)

run PaintProvider/scripts/paint_import.etcs pane=import_pane palette=palette menu=menu
menu.BindTool(@brush)
menu.BindImportPrompt(@palette, @import_pane, @import_input, @import_name)

# ── the scene, all at once, finished ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One line, and it is the line the stage wrapper exists for: before it there is
# a throbber on a dark page, after it there is a paint program, and no frame in
# between showing half of one. No Compose: stage has been the compose root since
# the top of this file, and everything above simply filled it in.
sheet_root.SetHidden(0)

# ── the layout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHY THERE IS ONE AT ALL. Every size and position above this line was a number
# typed against 1024x768, and half of them were that number read backwards --
# the bar at y=704 because 768-64, the throbber at 464,336 because the centre.
# A window that is any other size makes every one of them wrong, which is why
# the canvas was pinned to 4:3 by the page's CSS: the shape could not change
# because the scene could not follow it.
#
# So the boxes that have to follow are declared here and the solver answers,
# writing back through MoveTo and ResizeTo (Clayout.h). The document is NOT one
# of them and must not be: a picture is 1024x768 whatever the window is, and
# PaintSurface already blits it into the pane with a zoom and a pan. Growing
# the pane gives the picture more room around it; it does not stretch it.
#
# kinds: 0 fit, 1 grow, 2 fixed, 3 percent. parent 0 is the root.
#
# Declared LAST because a box has to exist to be named, and solved once here so
# the scene is right at the size we came up at -- FollowResize then hands the
# window the reins, and re-solves where the size arrives rather than on a tick.
# The solver itself is spawned further up, where the toolbar can reach it.

# The sheet is the whole window, and the stage is the sheet -- one column each,
# because the wrapper exists to switch the throbber against the scene and not
# to arrange anything (see THE STAGE, above).
layout.AddBox(@stage, 0, 1, 0.0, 1, 0.0)
layout.SetDirection(@stage, 1)
layout.AddBox(@sheet_root, @stage, 1, 0.0, 1, 0.0)
layout.SetDirection(@sheet_root, 1)

# THE MARGIN IS THE PADDING NOW, and it is the same 36px the inset was -- the
# band the ruler draws in, which is a property of the sheet rather than a
# subtraction the pane has to remember. The bar is inside it too, which the
# full-bleed backing strip used to hide; the band now runs round the toolbar as
# well as the picture.
layout.SetPadding(@sheet_root, 36.0)
layout.SetGap(@sheet_root, 8.0)

# The picture takes everything the two fixed rows under it do not.
layout.AddBox(@paper_pane, @sheet_root, 1, 0.0, 1, 0.0)
layout.AddBox(@zoom_pane, @sheet_root, 1, 0.0, 2, 26.0)
layout.AddBox(@bar, @sheet_root, 1, 0.0, 2, 64.0)
# THE BAR IS A ROW, and this is what makes the toolbar's sixteen cells share the
# width instead of stopping at 960. AFTER its AddBox and not before: SetDirection
# finds the box or does nothing, and AddBox defaults a new one to a column -- so
# stated earlier this line is silently dropped and the bar lays its cells out
# vertically, sixteen four-pixel strips in a 64px bar.
layout.SetDirection(@bar, 0)

layout.Solve()
layout.FollowResize(@main)

# NOTHING RE-RENDERS HERE, and that is deliberate. The solve above resized the
# pane, but a compositor applies an extent on its next recompose rather than
# when it is asked (CompositeDrawable2D::ResizeTo) -- so a Render on this line
# would draw the view, and the ruler's band with it, for the size the pane is
# about to stop having. The surface watches its own pane for exactly this and
# redraws a frame later (PaintSurface::AnimatingConcrete).

# ── the pumps ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The frame edge is not here: it is detached at the top of this file, and there
# is one per surface.
detach paint_routed_pointer.etcs window=main router=router

main.ProduceEvents() -> router.ConsumeInput()
